{"module":"culture","voice":"female","duration":185.8,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Sports, music and culture","start":0.0,"end":2.71,"words":[[0.0,0.63],[0.99,1.62],[1.62,1.93],[1.93,2.55]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Sports and music are where many Americans first meet Black excellence, and where the color line was most visible, challenged and crossed.","start":3.51,"end":11.69,"words":[[3.51,3.86],[3.86,4.07],[4.07,4.5],[4.5,4.57],[4.57,4.78],[4.78,5.07],[5.07,5.71],[5.71,5.99],[5.99,6.21],[6.21,6.49],[6.49,7.13],[7.38,7.59],[7.59,7.81],[7.81,7.95],[7.95,8.23],[8.23,8.52],[8.52,8.73],[8.73,9.08],[9.08,9.65],[9.9,10.54],[10.54,10.76],[10.76,11.18]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Jack Johnson","start":12.04,"end":13.12,"words":[[12.09,12.42],[12.42,13.01]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Jack Johnson became the first Black world heavyweight boxing champion in 1908.","start":13.82,"end":19.47,"words":[[13.86,14.16],[14.16,14.69],[14.69,15.14],[15.14,15.29],[15.29,15.6],[15.6,15.9],[15.9,16.2],[16.2,16.8],[16.8,17.25],[17.25,17.86],[17.86,18.01],[18.01,18.99]]},{"el":"s0","text":"When he beat the white former champion James Jeffries in 1910, white mobs attacked Black Americans in cities across the country.","start":19.82,"end":28.08,"words":[[19.87,20.1],[20.1,20.25],[20.25,20.48],[20.48,20.63],[20.63,20.93],[20.93,21.23],[21.23,21.83],[21.83,22.28],[22.28,22.81],[22.81,22.96],[22.96,23.78],[24.05,24.35],[24.35,24.65],[24.65,25.02],[25.02,25.32],[25.32,26.0],[26.0,26.15],[26.15,26.53],[26.53,26.9],[26.9,27.05],[27.05,27.58]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In 1913 he was convicted under the Mann Act, widely seen as a racially motivated prosecution, and he was pardoned posthumously in 2018.","start":28.43,"end":38.7,"words":[[28.48,28.63],[28.63,29.5],[29.5,29.65],[29.65,29.87],[29.87,30.53],[30.53,30.82],[30.82,30.97],[30.97,31.19],[31.19,31.48],[31.74,32.18],[32.18,32.4],[32.4,32.55],[32.55,32.69],[32.69,33.21],[33.21,34.01],[34.01,34.89],[35.15,35.37],[35.37,35.52],[35.52,35.74],[35.74,36.18],[36.18,37.13],[37.13,37.28],[37.28,38.23]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Breaking the color line","start":39.6,"end":41.16,"words":[[39.64,40.22],[40.22,40.38],[40.38,40.71],[40.71,41.04]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Jesse Owens won four gold medals at the 1936 Berlin Olympics.","start":41.86,"end":47.37,"words":[[41.91,42.32],[42.32,42.73],[42.73,42.98],[42.98,43.15],[43.15,43.56],[43.56,44.05],[44.05,44.22],[44.22,44.38],[44.38,45.62],[45.62,46.03],[46.03,46.77]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Jackie Robinson broke Major League Baseball's color line in 1947, enduring threats and abuse.","start":47.72,"end":55.53,"words":[[47.77,48.18],[48.18,48.82],[48.82,49.23],[49.23,49.71],[49.71,49.95],[49.95,50.6],[50.6,50.92],[50.92,51.25],[51.25,51.41],[51.41,52.78],[53.06,53.79],[53.79,54.2],[54.2,54.44],[54.44,54.92]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Before him, Black players starred in the Negro Leagues; in 2020 MLB recognized those leagues as major leagues, and in 2024 it added their statistics to the official records, making Josh Gibson the career batting-average leader.","start":55.88,"end":70.64,"words":[[55.93,56.22],[56.22,56.51],[56.76,57.05],[57.05,57.48],[57.48,57.78],[57.78,57.92],[57.92,58.07],[58.07,58.5],[58.5,58.86],[59.12,59.26],[59.26,60.13],[60.13,60.57],[60.57,61.29],[61.29,61.58],[61.58,61.87],[61.87,62.02],[62.02,62.45],[62.45,62.82],[63.07,63.29],[63.29,63.43],[63.43,64.45],[64.45,64.59],[64.59,64.88],[64.88,65.1],[65.1,65.82],[65.82,65.97],[65.97,66.12],[66.12,66.55],[66.55,67.06],[67.31,67.75],[67.75,68.04],[68.04,68.47],[68.47,68.62],[68.62,68.98],[68.98,69.78],[69.78,70.14]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Athletes who protested","start":71.54,"end":73.32,"words":[[71.59,72.15],[72.15,72.34],[72.34,73.18]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Muhammad Ali refused to be drafted in 1967, was stripped of his title and convicted, and won his case at the Supreme Court in 1971.","start":74.02,"end":84.24,"words":[[74.07,74.57],[74.57,74.79],[74.79,75.29],[75.29,75.43],[75.43,75.58],[75.58,76.08],[76.08,76.22],[76.22,77.59],[77.84,78.05],[78.05,78.48],[78.48,78.63],[78.63,78.84],[78.84,79.27],[79.27,79.49],[79.49,80.21],[80.46,80.67],[80.67,80.89],[80.89,81.1],[81.1,81.39],[81.39,81.53],[81.53,81.68],[81.68,82.11],[82.11,82.32],[82.32,82.47],[82.47,83.76]]},{"el":"s2","text":"At the 1968 Olympics, Tommie Smith and John Carlos raised gloved fists on the medal stand; Australian silver medalist Peter Norman wore a human rights badge in solidarity.","start":84.59,"end":97.51,"words":[[84.64,84.8],[84.8,84.96],[84.96,86.34],[86.34,87.08],[87.36,87.69],[87.69,88.01],[88.01,88.26],[88.26,88.58],[88.58,89.07],[89.07,89.48],[89.48,89.88],[89.88,90.29],[90.29,90.45],[90.45,90.62],[90.62,91.02],[91.02,91.51],[91.79,92.61],[92.61,93.02],[93.02,93.67],[93.67,93.99],[93.99,94.4],[94.4,94.56],[94.56,94.72],[94.72,95.21],[95.21,95.62],[95.62,95.94],[95.94,96.11],[96.11,97.0]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 2016 quarterback Colin Kaepernick knelt during the national anthem to protest police violence; he has not played in the NFL since 2017 and settled a grievance with the league in 2019.","start":97.86,"end":110.46,"words":[[97.91,98.05],[98.05,98.97],[98.97,99.54],[99.54,99.89],[99.89,100.39],[100.39,100.67],[100.67,101.17],[101.17,101.31],[101.31,101.81],[101.81,102.16],[102.16,102.3],[102.3,102.87],[102.87,103.23],[103.23,103.86],[104.11,104.25],[104.25,104.47],[104.47,104.68],[104.68,105.03],[105.03,105.18],[105.18,105.32],[105.32,105.74],[105.74,106.03],[106.03,107.02],[107.02,107.23],[107.23,107.66],[107.66,107.8],[107.8,108.29],[108.29,108.51],[108.51,108.65],[108.65,108.86],[108.86,109.0],[109.0,110.0]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 2020, WNBA players helped elect a challenger to their own team's co-owner after she criticized the league's racial justice stance.","start":110.81,"end":119.72,"words":[[110.86,111.0],[111.0,111.91],[112.15,113.14],[113.14,113.56],[113.56,113.91],[113.91,114.26],[114.26,114.4],[114.4,115.03],[115.03,115.17],[115.17,115.38],[115.38,115.59],[115.59,115.87],[115.87,116.37],[116.37,116.65],[116.65,116.79],[116.79,117.49],[117.49,117.63],[117.63,117.91],[117.91,118.33],[118.33,118.82],[118.82,119.24]]},{"el":"h3","text":"The Black roots of American music","start":120.62,"end":122.83,"words":[[120.67,120.83],[120.83,121.14],[121.14,121.45],[121.45,121.61],[121.61,122.24],[122.24,122.71]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Spirituals, the blues, jazz, gospel, rhythm and blues, rock and roll (Sister Rosetta Tharpe was an early pioneer), funk and hip-hop, born at a party in the Bronx in 1973, all came out of Black communities.","start":123.53,"end":139.46,"words":[[123.58,124.53],[124.8,124.96],[124.96,125.36],[125.64,126.03],[126.31,126.86],[127.14,127.54],[127.54,127.77],[127.77,128.17],[128.45,128.68],[128.68,128.92],[128.92,129.24],[129.24,129.63],[129.63,130.19],[130.19,130.43],[130.43,130.66],[130.66,130.82],[130.82,131.06],[131.06,131.69],[131.97,132.29],[132.29,132.52],[132.52,133.08],[133.35,133.59],[133.59,133.75],[133.75,133.91],[133.91,134.23],[134.23,134.38],[134.38,134.54],[134.54,135.02],[135.02,135.18],[135.18,136.6],[136.88,137.04],[137.04,137.35],[137.35,137.59],[137.59,137.75],[137.75,138.07],[138.07,138.94]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Billie Holiday's 1939 recording of “Strange Fruit” brought the horror of lynching to a national audience.","start":139.81,"end":147.04,"words":[[139.85,140.16],[140.16,140.78],[140.78,141.95],[141.95,142.5],[142.5,142.66],[142.66,143.28],[143.28,143.59],[143.59,143.91],[143.91,144.06],[144.06,144.37],[144.37,144.53],[144.53,145.08],[145.08,145.23],[145.23,145.39],[145.39,145.93],[145.93,146.48]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Many Black musicians of the early record industry were paid flat fees and lost their royalties and rights.","start":147.39,"end":154.44,"words":[[147.44,147.78],[147.78,148.11],[148.11,148.86],[148.86,149.03],[149.03,149.2],[149.2,149.45],[149.45,149.87],[149.87,150.54],[150.54,150.71],[150.71,151.04],[151.04,151.38],[151.38,151.63],[151.63,151.88],[151.88,152.22],[152.22,152.47],[152.47,153.14],[153.14,153.39],[153.39,153.89]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Appreciation or appropriation?","start":155.34,"end":157.8,"words":[[155.39,156.23],[156.23,156.29],[156.29,157.34]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Big Mama Thornton's 1953 recording of “Hound Dog” was a hit on the rhythm-and-blues charts; Elvis Presley's 1956 version sold millions and made him a star, while she earned little.","start":158.5,"end":170.99,"words":[[158.57,158.79],[158.79,159.09],[159.09,159.61],[159.61,160.73],[160.73,161.26],[161.26,161.41],[161.41,161.78],[161.78,162.0],[162.0,162.23],[162.23,162.38],[162.38,162.6],[162.6,162.75],[162.75,162.9],[162.9,163.8],[163.8,164.25],[164.51,164.88],[164.88,165.4],[165.4,166.6],[166.6,166.97],[166.97,167.35],[167.35,167.87],[167.87,168.09],[168.09,168.39],[168.39,168.62],[168.62,168.77],[168.77,169.06],[169.33,169.63],[169.63,169.77],[169.77,170.0],[170.0,170.45]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Some see stories like this as theft that left Black artists poor while others profited; others see cultural exchange that spread Black art.","start":171.34,"end":181.15,"words":[[171.39,171.66],[171.66,171.83],[171.83,172.36],[172.36,172.71],[172.71,172.98],[172.98,173.15],[173.15,173.51],[173.51,173.77],[173.77,174.12],[174.12,174.48],[174.48,175.01],[175.01,175.27],[175.27,175.62],[175.62,175.98],[175.98,176.77],[177.08,177.43],[177.43,177.61],[177.61,178.4],[178.4,179.28],[179.28,179.55],[179.55,179.99],[179.99,180.34],[180.34,180.61]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Most agree that credit and pay should follow the art.","start":181.5,"end":184.94,"words":[[181.55,181.94],[181.94,182.24],[182.24,182.47],[182.47,182.94],[182.94,183.17],[183.17,183.4],[183.4,183.63],[183.63,184.01],[184.01,184.17],[184.17,184.4]]}]}